+**Interface:** OpenAPI. Firecrawl's official v2 API document, https://docs.firecrawl.dev/api-reference/v2-openapi.json, served against https://api.firecrawl.dev/v2. Tools are generated live from it. Scrape, search, map, the developer and government index searches and the crawl-parameter preview are reads that Firecrawl models as POST; they are queries and run without approval, though scrapes and searches spend credits. A scrape or search still asks when its options can act on a site or persist state: browser actions other than wait, screenshot, scroll, scrape and pdf, change tracking, a browser profile that saves changes, or Alexandria provider tools. Every other POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE (crawls, batch scrapes, extracts, agents, monitors, interactive browser sessions, file parsing, the support agent, feedback and settings) is a mutation and asks for approval.
 
 **Auth:** API key. Create one in the Firecrawl dashboard at https://www.firecrawl.dev/app/api-keys (linked from the docs' introduction); it is sent as a bearer token. Firecrawl does publish OAuth metadata (https://www.firecrawl.dev/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server and /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource: public client, PKCE S256, dynamic client registration at /api/oauth/register, scopes firecrawl:global and offline_access, resource https://api.firecrawl.dev/), but OAuth is withheld: its auth.md page does not describe a standard OAuth flow, the other sources list only API keys, and it could not be verified here that an issued fco_ token works against api.firecrawl.dev/v2 or that Executor's connect flow can obtain a dynamically registered client. Revisit once that can be tested end to end.
 
